<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chilperic I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 539–584</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Chilperic I of Neustria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chilperic I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> who ruled the western kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> from 561 until his murder in 584. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>, he fought long wars against his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a>, and his feud with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, waged with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a> against Queen Brunhild, dominated Frankish politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> portrayed him as cruel and greedy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chilperic-i.jpg" alt="Chilperic I" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: elsen.eu · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremissis_-_Chilperic_I_-_Vermand_-_Transparent_Ver.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On the death of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> in 561, Chilperic tried to take the whole kingdom, seized the treasure at Berny and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His brothers compelled him to divide it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, Cambrai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therouanne">Thérouanne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>, fell to him, while Charibert received Paris, Guntram Burgundy and Sigebert Austrasia. When Charibert died in 567 the brothers divided his kingdom and agreed to share Paris and the territory around it.</p><p>Chilperic soon went to war with Sigebert. While Sigebert was away fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, Chilperic attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> and took it, but Sigebert returned, retook the city, defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> and imprisoned his eldest son Theudebert. The war flared again in 567 and in 573, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a> first allied with Chilperic and then changed sides, and Chilperic lost both times.</p><p>Sigebert married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunhilda-of-austrasia">Brunhilda</a>, daughter of the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanagild">Athanagild</a>. Chilperic, who had repudiated his first wife Audovera and taken a serving-woman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a>, as concubine, dismissed Fredegund and married Brunhilda's sister Galswintha. She was soon found strangled in her bed, and a few days later Chilperic married Fredegund.</p><p>The murder led to long wars between the brothers. In December 575 two assassins working for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fredegund">Fredegund</a> killed Sigebert, when he had Chilperic at his mercy. Chilperic then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> and places in Aquitaine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a> and encouraged discord there during the minority of Childebert II.</p><p>In 578 he sent an army against the Breton ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waroch-ii">Waroch II</a> of the Bro-Wened, who submitted after three days of fighting, did homage for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, gave his son as hostage and agreed to pay tribute. He later broke his oath, but Chilperic's dominion over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> was relatively secure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most of what is known of Chilperic comes from the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, who detested him, calling him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> and Herod of his time. He had provoked Gregory by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, seizing church property and appointing as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> counts of the palace who were not clerics. Gregory also objected to his attempts to teach a new doctrine of the Trinity, although some scholars dispute how far Gregory disliked him, and he reports a forced conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. His reign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> saw the introduction of the Byzantine punishment of eye-gouging.</p><p>Chilperic was also a musician of some talent, wrote verse modelled on that of Sedulius, tried to reform the Frankish alphabet, and worked to reduce the worst effects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> upon women. He was stabbed to death by an unknown assailant on his return from hunting at Chelles and buried in the Saint Vincent Basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Chlothar II</a> succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and later became sole king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chilperic I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Waroch repeatedly raided Frankish territory around Rennes and Nantes and defeated or bought off Frankish armies sent by Guntram and <b>Chilperic I</b> in the 570s and 580s.</blockquote><a href="/state/broerec">Broërec</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Chilperic I</b> received Soissons, the core of Neustria.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-soissons">Frankish Kingdom of Soissons</a></li><li><blockquote>Neustria, the new western land, took shape after the division of 561, when <b>Chilperic I</b> received Soissons and the lands between the Loire and the Scheldt.</blockquote><a href="/state/neustria">Neustria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Chelles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.88,2.59&amp;year=568" aria-label="Chelles on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4610 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Chilperic I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5060 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5060 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=48.88,2.59&amp;year=568">What was here in 568 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
